<h3>CHAPTER 1: Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Physics & Signal Generation</h3>

<p>Magnetic Resonance Imaging relies on the physical behavior of hydrogen nuclei (protons) in the presence of strong external magnetic fields. Hydrogen is chosen due to its high abundance in human tissue (water and fat) and its high gyromagnetic ratio.</p>

<h4>1.1 Fundamental NMR Principles</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Net Magnetization Vector (M0):</strong> In a main static magnetic field (B0), random proton spins align parallel or anti-parallel to B0, generating a macroscopic net magnetization vector aligned with the longitudinal z-axis.</li>
  <li><strong>Larmor Equation:</strong> Precession frequency ($\omega$) is directly proportional to B0 strength:
    <p style="text-align: center; font-weight: bold;">$\omega_0 = \gamma \times B_0$</p>
    Where $\gamma$ is the gyromagnetic ratio for hydrogen (42.58 MHz/Tesla). At 1.5 Tesla, precessional frequency is ~63.87 MHz; at 3.0 Tesla, it is ~127.74 MHz.</li>
  <li><strong>Radiofrequency (RF) Excitation:</strong> Applying an RF pulse at the exact Larmor frequency tips M0 away from the longitudinal axis into the transverse xy-plane, inducing a detectable current in the receiver coil (Free Induction Decay - FID).</li>
</ul>

