<h3>CHAPTER 2: Relaxation Times (T1, T2 & T2*) & Image Weighting Principles</h3>

<p>Once the RF excitation pulse stops, protons release absorbed energy and return to equilibrium. Image contrast is created by exploiting differences in relaxation rates across various tissues.</p>

<h4>2.1 T1 vs. T2 Relaxation Mechanics</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Relaxation Parameter</th>
      <th>Physical Process</th>
      <th>Key Tissue Behaviors</th>
      <th>Optimal Timing Parameters</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>T1 Relaxation (Spin-Lattice)</strong></td>
      <td>Recovery of longitudinal magnetization along B0 axis as energy transfers to surrounding lattice.</td>
      <td>Fat relaxes quickly (bright/hyperintense). Fluid relaxes slowly (dark/hypointense).</td>
      <td>Short Repetition Time (TR &lt; 800 ms)<br/>Short Echo Time (TE &lt; 30 ms)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>T2 Relaxation (Spin-Spin)</strong></td>
      <td>Decay of transverse magnetization due to irreversible spin-spin phase coherence loss.</td>
      <td>Fluid remains coherent longer (bright/hyperintense). Solid tissue dephases faster (intermediate/dark).</td>
      <td>Long Repetition Time (TR &gt; 2000 ms)<br/>Long Echo Time (TE &gt; 80 ms)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>T2* Relaxation</strong></td>
      <td>Transverse decay combined with local magnetic field inhomogeneities (B0 non-uniformity).</td>
      <td>Extremely sensitive to paramagnetic substances (acute blood, hemosiderin, metallic deposits).</td>
      <td>Gradient Echo (GRE) sequences without 180° refocusing pulse.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h4>2.2 Summary of Contrast Appearance in Main Brain Tissues</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Tissue Type</th>
      <th>T1-Weighted Image</th>
      <th>T2-Weighted Image</th>
      <th>Proton Density (PD) Image</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)</strong></td>
      <td>Dark (Hypointense)</td>
      <td>Bright (Hyperintense)</td>
      <td>Isointense / Slightly Bright</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>White Matter</strong></td>
      <td>Bright (Hyperintense)</td>
      <td>Dark (Hypointense)</td>
      <td>Intermediate</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Grey Matter</strong></td>
      <td>Intermediate / Dark</td>
      <td>Intermediate / Bright</td>
      <td>Intermediate / Bright</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Fat / Bone Marrow</strong></td>
      <td>Bright (Hyperintense)</td>
      <td>Intermediate / Bright</td>
      <td>Bright (Hyperintense)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

