<h3>CHAPTER 3: Pulse Sequences: Spin Echo, Gradient Echo, FLAIR & STIR</h3>

<p>Pulse sequences use combinations of RF pulses and spatial encoding magnetic gradients to emphasize specific tissue characteristics and minimize artifacts.</p>

<h4>3.1 Major Pulse Sequence Categories</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Conventional Spin Echo (SE):</strong> Uses a 90° excitation pulse followed by a 180° refocusing pulse to correct magnetic field inhomogeneities. Highly accurate, gold standard signal, but longer acquisition time.</li>
  <li><strong>Fast / Turbo Spin Echo (FSE/TSE):</strong> Applies multiple 180° refocusing pulses per TR interval (Echo Train Length - ETL), dramatically accelerating scan time.</li>
  <li><strong>Gradient Recalled Echo (GRE):</strong> Uses flip angles &lt; 90° and magnetic gradient reversals instead of 180° RF pulses. Faster scans, sensitive to T2* decay, hemorrhages, and microbleeds.</li>
</ul>

<h4>3.2 Suppression Sequences (FLAIR & STIR)</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Sequence Name</th>
      <th>Mechanism & Inversion Time (TI)</th>
      <th>Clinical Indications</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>FLAIR (Fluid Attenuated Inversion Recovery)</strong></td>
      <td>Long TI (~2000–2500 ms at 1.5T) set to null high signal from free water / CSF.</td>
      <td>Periventricular lesions, multiple sclerosis plaques, subarachnoid hemorrhage, meningitis.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>STIR (Short Tau Inversion Recovery)</strong></td>
      <td>Short TI (~140–180 ms at 1.5T) set to null signal from adipose tissue (fat).</td>
      <td>Bone marrow edema, optic neuritis, musculoskeletal soft tissue inflammation, trauma.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

